<p>You can sell a fire damaged house in Texas three ways: repair it and list it on the open market, list it as-is with an agent, or sell it to a cash buyer or investor. The right choice depends on how bad the damage is, how much insurance money you have, what your lender requires and how fast you need to move. This guide walks through each option, the insurance and mortgage issues that trip sellers up, and the disclosure rules you cannot skip.</p>

<p>If the fire just happened, start with our <a href="/blog/what-to-do-after-a-house-fire/">step-by-step checklist for what to do after a house fire</a>. Securing the property and opening the claim come before any sale decision.</p>

<h2>Step one: get a clear picture before you decide</h2>
<p>Sellers often decide too early, before they know what the damage and the insurance claim are really worth. Spend a few weeks gathering facts first. Every option below gets easier to compare once you have numbers on paper.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A written repair estimate</strong> from at least one licensed contractor who handles fire and smoke restoration, covering structure, electrical, HVAC, roof and smoke odor.</li>
<li><strong>Your insurance adjuster's estimate</strong> and a copy of your full policy, including the declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>Your mortgage payoff amount</strong> and the lender's process for releasing insurance funds.</li>
<li><strong>An as-is value and an after-repair value</strong> from a local agent who pulls comparable sales.</li>
<li><strong>Any fire marshal or city report</strong> and permit requirements for rebuilding.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep all of it in one place. A scanner that turns contractor estimates and adjuster letters into searchable PDFs saves real time later, both for the claim and for buyers' questions.</p>
[[product:document-scanner]]

<h2>Option 1: Repair the house, then list it</h2>
<p>Repairing first usually produces the highest sale price, because the house competes with normal listings and qualifies for standard mortgage financing. Buyers using FHA, VA or conventional loans need a property that meets the lender's condition standards, and a home with open fire damage often will not.</p>
<p>The trade-offs are time, cash and stress. Restoration can take months, you keep paying the mortgage, taxes, insurance and utilities while the work happens, and you manage contractors. Cost overruns are common once walls are opened and hidden smoke or water damage shows up.</p>
<p>Repairing makes the most sense when the damage is limited to part of the house, the insurance payout covers most of the work, and you are not under time pressure. It is also the path that unlocks any depreciation your insurer held back, which we cover below.</p>

<h2>Option 2: List it as-is on the open market</h2>
<p>You can list a fire damaged house with an agent and market it as-is. This reaches investors, flippers and owner-occupant buyers who want a project and may be paying cash or using renovation loans. More buyers competing often means a better price than a single direct offer.</p>
<p>"As-is" does not mean "no questions." Buyers will still inspect, still negotiate during their option period, and you still owe the disclosure notice. Our guide to <a href="/blog/selling-a-house-as-is-texas/">selling a house as-is in Texas</a> explains how the standard Texas contract handles this.</p>
<p>Expect a longer time on market than a repaired home, and price the listing realistically. Buyers will subtract their own repair estimate, a contingency for surprises and a profit margin from the after-repair value.</p>

<h2>Option 3: Sell to a cash buyer or investor</h2>
<p>Cash buyers and "we buy houses" companies will make an offer on almost any condition, often close in a few weeks, and typically skip financing contingencies. That speed matters if you cannot afford two housing payments, the house is a hazard, or the city is pressing you to secure or demolish it.</p>
<p>The cost is price. A cash offer on a fire damaged property is built backward from the after-repair value, minus repairs, holding costs, closing costs and the buyer's profit. Protect yourself with these habits:</p>
<ul>
<li>Get at least two or three written offers, not one.</li>
<li>Ask who pays closing costs and title fees, and whether there are any "service" or "admin" fees.</li>
<li>Be wary of a high initial offer that drops after the buyer's inspection.</li>
<li>Use a Texas title company for closing and confirm the earnest money is deposited with it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Comparing your options side by side</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Repair and list</th><th>List as-is</th><th>Cash buyer</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sale price</td><td>Highest</td><td>Middle</td><td>Lowest</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time to close</td><td>Longest (repairs plus marketing)</td><td>Weeks to months</td><td>Often a few weeks</td></tr>
