<tr><td>Commission</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Usually none</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Run each scenario through our <a href="/tools/seller-net-proceeds-calculator/">seller net proceeds calculator</a> so you compare what lands in your pocket, not the headline price.</p>

<h2>How the insurance payout interacts with the sale</h2>
<p>The insurance claim and the house sale are two separate deals, and you need to decide how they fit together before you sign a contract.</p>
<h3>Actual cash value vs. replacement cost</h3>
<p>Many homeowners policies settle dwelling damage in two stages. The insurer first pays actual cash value, which is the repair cost minus depreciation. The withheld depreciation is paid later, often only after the repairs are completed. If you sell without repairing, you may never collect that second payment. Check your policy wording and use our <a href="/tools/insurance-claim-depreciation-calculator/">insurance claim depreciation calculator</a> to see how much is at stake.</p>
<h3>Texas total-loss rule for fire</h3>
<p>Texas has a valued policy law for fire. Under Texas Insurance Code Section 862.053, when real property suffers a total loss by fire, the fire policy is treated as a liquidated demand for the full policy amount. That rule does not apply to personal property, and whether a home is a "total loss" is a fact question that insurers sometimes dispute. If your house is close to a total loss, get professional help before you accept a settlement.</p>
<h3>Claim deadlines insurers must meet</h3>
<p>Texas prompt-payment rules in Insurance Code Chapter 542 generally require an insurer to acknowledge a claim and begin investigating within 15 days of notice, accept or reject it within 15 business days after receiving everything it requested, and pay within 5 business days after accepting. Surplus lines carriers and suspected arson claims have longer timelines. Knowing these deadlines helps you plan a sale date.</p>
<h3>Who keeps the claim after closing?</h3>
<p>If the claim is still open when you sell, the contract should say whether you keep future payments, credit them to the buyer, or assign the claim to the buyer. Do not leave it vague. A real estate attorney can draft the assignment language, and a licensed public adjuster can help if you believe the claim is underpaid.</p>

<h2>Your mortgage company is part of the deal</h2>
<p>Almost every mortgage requires your lender to be named on the homeowners policy as mortgagee or loss payee. That is why dwelling checks typically arrive payable to both you and the lender. Under Texas Insurance Code Section 862.055, the mortgagee's interest in a fire policy generally is not invalidated by the owner's acts or neglect, so the lender's right to those funds is strong.</p>
<p>In practice, lenders handle large checks in one of a few ways. They may deposit the money in a loss-draft account and release it in stages as repairs are inspected, or they may apply it toward the loan balance. Call the lender's loss-draft department early and ask:</p>
<ol>
<li>How will you release funds if I repair before selling?</li>
<li>What happens to held funds if I sell the house unrepaired?</li>
<li>Will you apply the funds to my payoff at closing?</li>
</ol>
<p>Keep making mortgage payments while you sort this out. Missing payments after a fire can turn a damaged-property sale into a foreclosure problem.</p>

<h2>Disclosure duties when selling after a fire</h2>
<p>Texas Property Code Section 5.008 requires sellers of most single-family resales to give the buyer a written disclosure notice. The statutory form asks directly whether you are aware of "Previous Fires," along with water damage, needed repairs and work done without required permits. Firefighting can leave water damage behind, so answer those lines carefully too.</p>
<p>Disclose the fire, the date, what was damaged, what was repaired, who did the work and whether permits were pulled. Attach invoices, the adjuster's estimate and any remediation reports. Clear records reassure buyers and protect you if questions come up after closing. Store originals somewhere safe from the next disaster.</p>
[[product:sentrysafe-fireproof]]
<p>Some transfers are exempt from the form, such as foreclosure sales and sales by an executor administering an estate. An exemption from the form is not permission to conceal a known defect. Our full guide to the <a href="/blog/texas-seller-disclosure-notice/">Texas seller disclosure notice</a> covers the form line by line. Because every claim and contract is different, confirm your specific situation with a Texas real estate attorney.</p>

<h2>Pricing a fire damaged house</h2>
<p>Pricing is where most fire damaged sales go wrong. Price too high and the listing sits while the house deteriorates. Price too low and you hand equity to the buyer.</p>
<p>A practical approach is to start from the after-repair value based on recent comparable sales nearby. Then subtract a realistic repair budget, a contingency for hidden damage, carrying costs during repairs, and the profit margin a buyer needs. Compare that number with your as-is comparable sales, if any exist in your area. Ask your agent to show you the math, not just a number.</p>
<p>Lingering smoke odor scares buyers more than the visible damage. Professional cleaning and odor treatment, debris removal and boarding up openings are often worth doing even if you sell as-is. Before showings, make sure working smoke and carbon monoxide alarms are installed. The disclosure notice asks about smoke detectors, and buyers notice.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>To sell a fire damaged house in Texas for the most money, get your repair estimate, insurance numbers and lender's rules on paper first. Then compare repairing and listing, listing as-is, and taking a cash offer by net proceeds, not sticker price. Settle in writing who keeps any open insurance money, and disclose the fire fully on the seller's notice. Doing those three things protects both your bottom line and your peace of mind long after closing.</p>
